<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Merge throttle for the Langfuse data plane.

  Why this file exists. `memory.xml` states its own purpose as "the server now refuses
  work under pressure instead of growing until the kernel kills it", and names the merge
  scheduler as "the main consumer to be throttled" — but it sets only
  `max_server_memory_usage`, so nothing ever throttled a merge. Raising that ceiling from
  1.6 GB to 3 GB on 2026-10-15 therefore did not close the loop, it postponed it. By
  2026-10-16 ClickHouse was being killed roughly once a minute, `RestartCount` 1101, each
  kill at anon-rss ≈ 4.05–4.12 GB against the 4 GB cgroup, with `MergeMutate` named as the
  process that invoked the OOM killer.

  Why the merge and not the ceiling. The ceiling has now been raised twice — 2 GB, then
  4 GB — and the workload grew to fill it both times. The reason is that the memory a
  merge needs but ClickHouse does not track (thread stacks, allocator arenas, page cache
  over the parts being rewritten) scales with the size of the merge itself, so no fixed
  margin is large enough: whatever is left over is what the next merge tries to use.
  Bounding the merge bounds the growth. Raising the ceiling only moves the wall.

  REMOVED 2026-10-17 — `max_bytes_to_merge_at_once`, and it was never doing anything
  except preventing the server from starting. It is not a MergeTree setting in
  ClickHouse 25.12, and `sanityChecks` rejects it during startup:

      Code: 115. DB::Exception: Unknown setting 'max_bytes_to_merge_at_once':
      in MergeTree config. (UNKNOWN_SETTING)
      ... MergeTreeSettings::loadFromConfig ... sanityChecks ... Server::main

  That exception is fatal, so the process exits, Docker restarts it, and it exits
  again — a crash loop with a period of about 40-50 seconds. It is indistinguishable
  from the OOM loop this file was written to stop, by timing, by `RestartCount`, and
  by the fact that a query is refused. It was not that loop. `docker inspect` said
  `OOMKilled=false`, which was the truth rather than a Docker bug, and raising the
  cgroup cap from 4 GB to 5 GB on the same day changed nothing because memory was
  never the constraint any more.

  What made this hard to see is that the file's own claim is correct in the general
  case — a MergeTree setting belongs under `<merge_tree>` — and the nesting here was
  written exactly that way. The setting was simply removed from ClickHouse after the
  version this reasoning was formed against. The lesson is in the method, not the
  setting: the 2026-10-16 note that "the OOM loop continued unchanged" after this
  file landed was measured from OUTSIDE the container, by trying to query it. The
  container's own err log named the cause on the first line that mattered.

  `background_pool_size` is a SERVER setting, is valid at the root, and is what
  serialises merges. It stays.

  Still true, and still the durable fix: bounding merges was never the answer to a
  store that keeps growing. Retention is — Langfuse's own `CLICKHOUSE_TTL_DAYS`,
  applied through its migrations (Phase 3.1). This file now does only the one thing
  that is safe, and leaves the backlog to retention.

  Paired with: `memory.xml`, which holds the server ceiling. The lower of the two governs,
  so neither file should be changed without reading the other.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Intentionally empty.

      `background_pool_size=1` lived here too, and it is invalid against ClickHouse
      25.12's own defaults. With the pool at one, the server refuses to start
      because the mutation gate is set to 20 and a pool of one can never have
      twenty free entries:

          Code: 36. DB::Exception: The value of
          'number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation' setting (20)
          (default values are defined in <merge_tree> section of config.xml ...

      So both settings this file carried were fatal rather than merely
      ineffective, and each one masked the next — removing the unknown MergeTree
      setting is what let this one surface. A drop-in that fails a startup sanity
      check presents as an OOM loop: the process exits, Docker restarts it, and
      nothing about the timing or the restart counter distinguishes the two.

      The file is kept, empty, because the mount and this record are worth more
      than the two lines were. Anything added here later must be read back from
      `system.server_settings` or `system.merge_tree_settings` for the RUNNING
      version before it is committed, not taken from documentation for another one.

      The lever that actually bounds this store is retention, not merge sizing —
      see Phase 3.1 of `docs/langfuse_hardening_plan.md`.
    -->
</clickhouse>
